# Fohlioo — Project Overview

Fohlioo is an AI fashion intelligence platform. The core thesis: a browser
extension captures cross-brand shopper behavioural signals (dwell time,
scroll depth, wishlist adds, purchase patterns) and converts them into two
products:

1. **Shopper intelligence layer** — a personalised profile for the shopper
   themselves, built from their own behaviour across every fashion site
   they visit
2. **Brand analytics portal** — aggregated, anonymised behavioural
   intelligence sold to fashion brands (e.g. "23% of your customers are
   Investment Dressers who comparison-shop before buying")

Shoppers are classified into four behavioural archetypes derived entirely
from observed behaviour (never self-reported): Investment Dresser, Trend
Chaser, Quiet Minimalist, Brand Loyalist. See `docs/segmentation-model.md`
if present for the full classification logic — do not invent new segments
or thresholds without checking that doc first.

## The three repositories

This is one of three separate codebases. Know which one you're in and
don't blur their responsibilities:

| Repo | Purpose | Stack |
|---|---|---|
| **fohlioo-extension** | Chrome extension (Manifest V3). Captures product data + behavioural events on fashion sites. | Plasmo, TypeScript |
| **fohlioo-api** | Backend. Event ingestion, auth token exchange, (later) segmentation engine. | Hono, TypeScript, Supabase (Postgres + pgvector) |
| **fohlioo-app** (this repo) | Public-facing web app: landing page, signup/login, shopper dashboard, brand dashboard. | Next.js 14 (App Router), TypeScript, Tailwind, Supabase Auth |

If a task feels like it belongs in a different repo (e.g. "add a new event
type" belongs in fohlioo-extension + fohlioo-api, not here), say so rather
than building it in the wrong place.

## Non-negotiables

- **Behavioural data drives segmentation, not declared data.** Never add
  onboarding fields that ask shoppers to self-report style, size, or brand
  preference as a blocking signup step. That undermines the entire product
  thesis. Optional post-signup enrichment is fine.
- **Shopper and brand are two distinct user types** sharing one Supabase
  `auth.users` table but different `profiles`/`brand_accounts` shape and
  different dashboard routes. Don't merge their data models out of
  convenience.
- **The extension is the source of truth for anonymous activity.** Every
  install gets an `extension_id` before any account exists. Signup must
  never discard that shopper's pre-signup event history — see
  `20-auth-architecture.mdc` for the merge requirement.
